# spark in w3m -- your own AI, on your own machine (spark.forgewright.ai).
# Append this to your own ~/.w3m/keymap:
#
#     cat ~/.w3m/spark/keymap.spark >> ~/.w3m/keymap
#
# One key, spark's key in every app: M-s (Esc then s). The page you are
# reading stays put, and `spark> ` appears at the bottom of the screen:
#
#   Enter or ?         the overview: what does this page cover?
#   your words         your question ("does it mention prices"); a
#                      leading ? works too, the editors' habit
#   --part 2 words     a page past 16 kB answers with its part count;
#                      this reads part 2 (the answer names its part)
#   Ctrl-C             never mind (the answer buffer stays empty; B
#                      goes back to the page)
#
# The answer opens in a new buffer, every line quoting the page -- B
# goes back. spark-w3m is this repository's wrapper around `spark
# read`: it draws the prompt, folds spark's stderr into the answer (a
# refusal shows instead of vanishing) and wraps long lines at spaces
# (SPARK_W3M_WIDTH, default 78). Nothing leaves this machine but the
# rendered page text and your words, to the brain spark is configured
# for -- never the URL. Needs w3m 0.5.3 or newer and spark 1.20 or
# newer. M-s is a suggestion (w3m's own M-s, save buffer, moves aside)
# -- edit the line to taste.
keymap M-s PIPE_BUF "spark-w3m --ask"
